* macros.a99 - the stack and interrupt-service macros, shared by the loader,
* the kernel and every module.
*
*   .enter    a routine that calls another saves its return address
*   .leave    ... and takes it back (then RT).  .leave's MOV sets the
*             flags from R11, so results go in registers, not flags
*   .push Rn  / .pop Rn   save and restore a register on the software stack
*   .intsvc   let the console ISR run once (interrupts on, then off)
*   .mape/.mapf/.mapd Rn   map the page in Rn into window >E000 / >F000 /
*             >D000, inline (below): MAPE, MAPF and MAPD without the call
*   .callmod PAGE,N   call entry N of the module whose first page is
*             PAGE (MODCALL: R0 returns); N counts from 0: INIT 0, TICK 1,
*             REDRAW 2, reserved 3, your own entries from 4
*
* HOW THE SOFTWARE STACK WORKS.  The TMS9900 has no stack: BL puts the return
* address in R11, so a routine that calls another with BL would overwrite its
* own return address.  The convention here is that R10 is a stack pointer into
* a small area in slot A, growing downward.  .enter pushes R11 (DECT R10 makes
* room for one word; MOV R11,*R10 stores it) and .leave takes it back (MOV
* *R10+,R11 loads it and moves R10 up); .push and .pop do the same for any
* register.  A leaf routine (one that calls nothing) needs none of this.
* Rules that follow: a routine's .enter and .leave must balance on every path;
* the stack is only 128 words deep, so deep recursion overflows it silently
* into the data above; and .leave's MOV sets the status flags from R11, so a
* result must be returned in a register, never in the condition flags.
* The macro bodies are assembled in place wherever the macro name is used, so
* each .enter costs two instructions (6 bytes) and no call: macros are free of
* any call overhead.  '#1' and '#2' in a body stand for the first and second
* argument of the use.
       .defm enter
       DECT R10
       MOV  R11,*R10
       .endm
       .defm leave
       MOV  *R10+,R11
       .endm
       .defm push
       DECT R10
       MOV  #1,*R10
       .endm
       .defm pop
       MOV  *R10+,#1
       .endm
       .defm intsvc
       LIMI 2
       LIMI 0
       .endm
* .mape Rn - the page in Rn into window >E000, recorded in CURBE; .mapf Rn -
* the same for >F000 and CURBF.  Inline, about 10 instructions when the page
* changes (MAPE with its call and the saving round it is about 23) and 2
* when it is already there.  Rules: Rn is not R12 (the macro loads R12 with the
* mapper's CRU address and leaves it so); Rn holds the page number, 0-4095,
* and comes back as it was; the registers are written by this macro and
* nothing else, as MAPPG does, so the records stay right; never use it on
* the window the calling code is running in, which is the rule for MAPE too.
* Use MAPE and MAPF where speed does not matter: they are shorter.
       .defm mape
       C    #1,@CURBE
       JEQ  $+22
       MOV  #1,@CURBE
       LI   R12,AMSCRU
       SBO  0
       SWPB #1
       MOV  #1,@AMSREG+>1C
       SWPB #1
       SBZ  0
       .endm
       .defm mapf
       C    #1,@CURBF
       JEQ  $+22
       MOV  #1,@CURBF
       LI   R12,AMSCRU
       SBO  0
       SWPB #1
       MOV  #1,@AMSREG+>1E
       SWPB #1
       SBZ  0
       .endm
* .mapd Rn - the page in Rn into window >D000, recorded in CURCD: MAPD inline,
* with the same rules as .mape (Rn not R12, left as it was; R12 changes), and
* MAPD's own: for programs whose modules are single pages.
       .defm mapd
       C    #1,@CURCD
       JEQ  $+22
       MOV  #1,@CURCD
       LI   R12,AMSCRU
       SBO  0
       SWPB #1
       MOV  #1,@AMSREG+>1A
       SWPB #1
       SBZ  0
       .endm
       .defm callmod
       LI   R0,#1
       LI   R1,>C000+(2*#2)
       BL   @MODCALL
       .endm
